---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const title = "What no agreement can do";
const description =
  "Some things are outside what two people can agree between themselves — in every forum PMAP covers. Read this before paying anyone to draft around them.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>What no agreement can do</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Some things are outside what two people can agree between themselves, in every forum this
    product covers. A clause that tries is at best ineffective, and at worst evidence of bad faith
    that colours everything around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Child support and custody</h2>
  <p>
    Child support belongs to the child, follows statutory guidelines, and stays modifiable on a
    substantial change in circumstances. Custody and parenting time are decided on the child's best
    interests at the time. Neither can be set, capped, waived, or pre-committed by contract, and a
    clause purporting to do so is void.
  </p>

  <h2>Retirement plan survivor benefits</h2>
  <p>
    This is the one people miss, and it fails silently. A premarital agreement cannot waive a
    spouse's survivor annuity or death benefit in a 401(k), 403(b) or pension plan. Federal law lets
    only a <em>spouse</em> consent, and before the wedding neither of you is one. What works is a
    covenant in the agreement plus a consent signed on the plan's own form after the marriage,
    witnessed by a plan representative or a notary.
  </p>

  <h2>An immigration affidavit of support</h2>
  <p>
    A sponsor's Form I-864 obligation runs to the government and to the sponsored immigrant. Divorce
    does not end it, and an agreement between the spouses cannot waive it. Any support waiver is
    subject to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Needs, in a discretionary system</h2>
  <p>
    In England and Wales, Ireland and other needs-based systems, the needs of a spouse and of the
    children cannot be contracted away. An agreement that leaves one party in real need will be
    varied however carefully it was drafted.
  </p>

  <h2>Some local property rights</h2>
  <p>
    In Ontario, a marriage contract cannot limit a spouse's right to possession of the matrimonial
    home. In Quebec, the family patrimony survives any contrary clause. Florida homestead is
    constitutional and generally cannot be devised away from a spouse without a valid written
    waiver. Imported agreements routinely miss these.
  </p>

  <h2>Anything requiring concealment</h2>
  <p>
    Hiding an asset, backdating an instrument, holding property through a nominee to mislead the
    other party or a court, or transferring property to defeat a known creditor. A transfer made
    while insolvent or to defeat a known claim is voidable, and in bankruptcy it can cost the
    discharge. A last-minute transfer before a wedding is exactly the fact pattern a court reads as
    a badge of fraud.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this page exists</h2>
  <p>
    A void clause does not just fail — it invites a court to look harder at the rest of the
    agreement. A plan built on one of these assumptions has a hole in it that no amount of drafting
    closes, and it is much cheaper to find that out now than in a deposition. The honest versions of
    these goals work: a documented market-rate salary, an irrevocable trust funded before the
    marriage with an independent trustee, a disclosed and independently advised agreement, an
    updated estate plan.
  </p>

  <p><a href="/not-legal-advice/">None of this is legal advice.</a></p>
</SiteLayout>
